  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1103 PM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nClear skies and nearly calm winds overnight tonight. A surface\nboundary moving through bringing in cooler air coupled with good\nradiational cooling will help generate some fog mainly along the\nMissouri River Valley and southwest Iowa. Elsewhere should\nremain mostly clear. Any fog should burn off fairly soon after\nsunrise. \n\nLooking at the upper-level pattern we have broad ridging over\nthe West Coast and troughing over the Great Lakes into the\nNortheast. Through the week we see ridging gradually creep\neastward, mostly toward the end of the week with a couple of\nmid-level disturbances moving through Thursday and Friday.\n\nLower down we see high pressure building over the Great Plains\ntoday with a Canadian air mass bringing temperatures down across\nthe region. Highs today will be several degrees cooler for much\nof the area than yesterday, only in the upper 70s to low 80s \n(far south). This cooling trend continues into Thursday with\nincreasing northwesterly flow aloft as the ridge starts to shift\neastward. Highs on Thursday only get into the low-to-mid 70s.\n\nA weak disturbance best seen at h850 move through Thursday night\nwith increased WAA across our area. This brings in additional\nmoisture allowing for a few showers and storms to develop\nthrough the evening and overnight hours Thursday night-early\nFriday. These will mainly be confined to the nose of the LLJ\nalong the Missouri river in NE Nebraska into western Iowa.\nDespite the forcing, the development of these showers and storms\nwill greatly depend on how much moisture we can advect in. While\ndeterministic guidance and ensembles show potential for these\nshowers/storms, model soundings are still dry especially in the\nlower levels. It could end up being a scenario for more virga \nwith only a few sprinkles/brief light showers.\n\nGoing into the weekend we see a rapidly deepening trough along\nthe West Coast amplifying a jet streak over the four-corners\nregion. As this occurs we see a broad region of southwesterly\nflow develop over the Rockies into the Great Plains. This\ncombined with a substantial low pressure system moving across\nCentral Canada will lead to the development of a strong 850-mb\njet across the Northern and Central Plains. These strong \nsouthwesterly winds will mix down to the surface during the day\non Saturday leading to potential for 30-40 mph winds through \nthe afternoon. This could potentially lead to fire weather \nconcerns as agricultural fields have dried out, especially \nacross central and northeast Nebraska which didn't see as much \nrain over the last week. \n\nConfidence greatly decreases in the upper-level pattern forecast\nSunday into next week as we likely see a very baroclinic, active\npattern set up across the CONUS. Ensemble guidance support the \nreturn of rain chances starting Monday or Tuesday next week.",
